#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The differential harness (#70): the base build against the head on the hermetic suite.

    tools/differential                      build the base (the merge-base of HEAD and
                                            origin/beta) and the head, run both, diff
    tools/differential --base REV           the base at REV
    tools/differential --base-bin B --head-bin H
                                            take built binaries (CI passes artifacts);
                                            bsv-wallet-mcp is read beside each
    tools/differential --same [--head-bin H]
                                            the floor: one binary run twice
    tools/differential --ports A,B,C,F,S     use five explicit ports instead of
                                            choosing free ports for this pair
    tools/differential --compare R1 R2      diff two recordings already made
    tools/differential --exclusions         print the normalization list and exit

Each run is `E2E_HERMETIC=1 node tests/e2e/run.js` from this tree's harness (the
scenarios, the stub and its fixtures are the head's for both builds, so only the
binary differs), with DIFF_RECORD set (tests/e2e/lib/record.js writes every HTTP
call and CLI command as a JSON line) and DIFF_ROOT_KEYS set to four keys this tool
draws once per comparison, so both runs drive the same wallets on the same ports.
Ports are chosen once per pair. --compare can read recordings from separate
invocations, so their recorded ports and loopback URLs are normalized by role,
like wallet directories. A ports event carries the stub's port as well as the
wallet ports; it supplies normalization metadata, not a compared harness call.
Port normalization makes a cross-run comparison readable, but it is not a zero
test because the root keys differ; a zero test compares the pair within one run.

Base builds hold an exclusive fcntl.flock on <target>/.differential.lock through
the build and the copy of both binaries into the run's output directory.
A head build is copied there too;
each run's head CARGO_TARGET_DIR must be private so another build cannot replace
the binaries while they are being copied. Recordings use these copies, never
the build cache's paths.

The two recordings are compared byte for byte after the normalizations of
EXCLUSIONS, and only those; each names what it rewrites and why two runs of the
same code legitimately differ there. The report: per scenario, per call, the
earliest differing field with both values, and the line
`DIFFERENTIAL: 0 differences over N calls and M commands` (or the count).
Exit 0 on zero differences, 1 on differences, 2 when a build or a run failed,
3 when a recording is not a whole run (INCOMPLETE: no events, no wallets, a
missing setup, scenario or sweep phase, no HTTP calls or no commands, a line
that is not JSON, or a phase whose call count differs between the two): an
incomplete recording is never read as zero differences, nor as a difference.

docs/DIFFERENTIAL.md says how a lane runs it and what the label rule is. A Python
script with the standard library only, like tools/ratchet.
"""

